---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
	title="Software for community pottery studios"
	description="Booking software for community and membership pottery studios: wheel time by membership tier, shared kiln queues with firing fees, open-studio check-in and classes."
>
	<section class="band-dark page-hero">
		<div class="wrap split">
			<div>
				<p class="eyebrow">For community studios</p>
				<h1>One studio, a hundred members, three kilns.</h1>
				<p class="lede">
					Community studios run on shared everything: shared wheels, shared shelves, shared kilns.
					Kilnbook keeps track of who is using what, so you can spend open-studio hours on the floor
					instead of at the whiteboard.
				</p>
				<a href="/contact" class="btn">Book a demo</a>
			</div>
			<img
				src="/images/studio-floor.svg"
				alt="A community studio floor with rows of wheels, hand-building tables and a kiln room"
				width="560"
				height="360"
			/>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section section-alt">
		<div class="wrap">
			<p class="eyebrow">Sound familiar?</p>
			<h2>What membership studios tell us</h2>
			<div class="cards">
				<div class="card">
					<h3>The same wheels, every evening</h3>
					<p>
						A few members claim the good wheels at 5pm, and everyone else stands around waiting.
						Weekday mornings sit empty.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>"Is my bowl out yet?"</h3>
					<p>
						Pieces from dozens of members share the same bisque shelf. Nobody knows which load their
						work is in, and firing fees get worked out on a clipboard at the end of the month.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="card">
					<h3>Members, classes and drop-ins in three places</h3>
					<p>
						Memberships live in one tool, class sign-ups in another, and open-studio attendance in a
						notebook by the door.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="wrap section narrow">
		<h2>Fair wheel time for every tier</h2>
		<p>
			Members reserve wheels, hand-building tables and shared equipment in one-hour blocks. Give each
			membership tier its own weekly hours and booking window, make evenings and weekends count
			double, and set a cancellation window so no-shows free up slots for someone else. A tablet by
			the door handles open-studio check-in, so you always know who is in the building while the kilns
			are running. <a href="/features/wheel-time">How wheel time works</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Shared kilns without the clipboard</h2>
		<p>
			Members log a piece from their phone when they set it on the shelf and pay the firing fee by
			cubic inch or per piece. Each kiln keeps its own bisque and glaze queues, and when a load comes
			out, everyone with work in it gets a pickup text with a photo of the shelf.
			<a href="/features/firings">How firing queues work</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Classes that bring in new members</h2>
		<p>
			Most community studios grow through their classes. Sell six-week courses, drop-in sessions and
			class packs, and let waitlists fill cancelled seats on their own. Instructors see their roster
			and mark attendance from the same studio tablet.
			<a href="/features/classes">Classes and packs</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Which plan fits?</h2>
		<p>
			Most community studios choose the Studio plan at $79 a month. It covers up to 3 kilns with
			unlimited members, wheel and table reservations, class packs, waitlists and memberships, and
			pickup texts. Running more than three kilns or more than one location? Collective has no kiln
			limit. <a href="/pricing">Compare plans</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="cta-band">
		<div class="wrap">
			<h2>Give your members their evenings back.</h2>
			<p>30 days free on the Studio plan. No card to start, and nothing is deleted if you walk away.</p>
			<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-dark">Book a demo</a>
		</div>
	</section>
</Base>
